#
# repo-looper: the one looper of one tacplus-dev repository. It runs in the
# looper container, as a job of the GitLab looper pipeline on the autoscaled
# runner (one job per repository at a time), and only while the repository
# has work.
#
#   repo-looper --repo NAME             the parent: work NAME's ready issues, priority:high first, oldest
#                                       first within a priority, as many at once as it plans
#   repo-looper --repo NAME --issue N   the same, issue N first if it is still ready (what the pipeline runs)
#   repo-looper --repo NAME --scan      the nightly scan: file issues, change no code
#   repo-looper --repo NAME --list      print the ready issues; in a GitLab job, none while NAME
#                                       has a looper job queued, waiting or running there (the plan)
#   repo-looper --repo NAME --job-timeout N   the CI job timeout for issue N and the ready issues that
#                                       could run beside it (the longest limit, the plan's time and an hour)
#   repo-looper --repo NAME --issue N --checkpoint MSG   push the checkout's work in progress (the model's step)
#   repo-looper --repo NAME --issue N --land [--gates CMD]   land the child's commits on main (the model's
#                                       step): under the landing lock, rebased on main, gates re-run
#   repo-looper --repo NAME --issue N --think DIR   the think job of a looper with its own identity
#                                       (looper#28): no write credential, writes its outcome to DIR
#
# The parent and its children (looper#32): the job's repo-looper is the parent.
# It takes the ready issues in priority order and plans which can run together:
# not two that touch the same paths, generated outputs or contracts, or that
# the model, asked for each issue's areas, names as a conflict (an issue whose
# areas it cannot tell runs alone); as many as the runner's CPU and memory hold
# (LOOPER_CHILD_CPUS, default 2, and LOOPER_CHILD_MEMORY_GB, default 4, per
# child), never more than LOOPER_MAX_CHILDREN (default 6, a safety ceiling), and
# in a GitLab job none whose limit outlasts the job (CI_JOB_TIMEOUT from the
# job's start, less an hour). The plan and its reasons go to the job's log and its record
# (LOOPER_RUNS/<time>-plan), and to each issue it started beside others or held
# back. Each issue is a child: this same script (--child N) running the
# per-issue flow below in its own worktree, LOOPER_WORK/NAME/worktrees/N, on
# its own branch looper/NAME-N. Children land one at a time, by the parent
# (looper#34): --land queues the child's commits with the parent's lander, which
# takes the repository's landing lock, rebases onto the current main, re-runs
# the gates and pushes, even after the child has ended; a push to main from a
# child's worktree any other way is refused. In a GitLab job a land that would
# not finish before the job ends is not started (looper#38): it is deferred,
# its branch left land-pending, and the issue's next run lands it first.
# As each child ends the parent logs its outcome and plans again, so a newly
# ready issue starts while the others run; a child's time limit is its own.
#
# Ready: open, labeled `approved`, not held (looper:in-progress, looper:stuck,
# needs-decision), and every `Blocked-by:` issue in its body closed.
# With --issue N in a GitLab job, N labelled looper:in-progress but otherwise
# ready, and no other looper job of NAME active, N is taken as a resume: its run
# was killed outright (looper#20).
#
# One checkout per repository, kept across issues and runs under
# LOOPER_WORK/NAME/checkout, and the children's worktrees of it: the looper
# always works the same code base. Claude may run sub-agents in its worktree for
# independent parts; only the child's top-level run commits and lands, on main
# (pre-production rule, Jody 2026-10-26).
#
# A run whose job GitLab no longer runs (looper#21): GitLab can mark a job
# failed (runner_system_failure) while its container keeps working on the VM,
# and retry it. In a GitLab job the run polls its own job every
# LOOPER_JOB_POLL; once GitLab reports it anything but running (or refuses the
# job's token after accepting it), the run checkpoints and stops, pushing
# nothing else, commenting nothing and leaving the label to the retry, which
# resumes from that checkpoint. A run that cannot read GitLab keeps going.
#
# Each run's record (prompt.md, claude.log, outcome) is a directory under
# LOOPER_RUNS. The pipeline sets it inside the job's project directory and
# uploads it as the job's artifacts: the runner VM does not outlive the job.
#
# Never lose a run's work (tacplus-dev/looper#7): while the model works, its
# work in progress (commits, changes, new files) is checkpointed to the branch
# looper/NAME-N on origin, never main: whenever the model finishes a step
# (--checkpoint), every LOOPER_CHECKPOINT_INTERVAL, and when the model stops.
# A run on an issue whose branch exists starts from it, rebased on main (else
# main merged into it, its conflicts if any marked and named in the prompt), as
# uncommitted changes; the branch is deleted once the work lands on main.
#
# The run's limit is a line `Timeout: <hours>h` in the issue body (at most 10h),
# else LOOPER_TIMEOUT; the model is stopped LOOPER_STOP_EARLY seconds before it.
# In a GitLab job it is capped at the parent's deadline (the job's end less an
# hour), and the issue is told so (looper#35): the run stops by itself, before
# GitLab kills the job.
#
# A finished or idle run ends (tacplus-dev/looper#8): a watchdog stops the model
# when it went idle (no output and no change in the checkout for
# LOOPER_IDLE_MINUTES; the issue gets a comment with its log tail and is
# looper:stuck), and when the run is done (it closed the issue, or wrote its
# Blocked-by outcome) and the model has not exited LOOPER_DONE_GRACE seconds
# later. Every checkpoint also carries the run's record (prompt.md, the
# claude.log tail) under .looper/, so a cancelled run still leaves its log.
#
# After the run: closed or needs-decision is the run's own outcome. An issue
# that waits on other open issues (the run writes `Blocked-by: <issue>` lines
# to its outcome file, whether it pushed a fix whose gates wait or rightly
# pushed nothing, looper#22) gets those lines in its body and stays approved,
# so it is worked again once they close. A run that reached its limit, that
# ended with its land queued (the parent lands it), or that ended early having
# made progress (a fix landed, a new step checkpointed; looper#34), or that went
# idle after its fix landed (looper#37) says what is done and what is left and
# stays approved: the next run resumes from its branch. Anything else (idle
# with nothing landed or queued, no progress) is looper:stuck, for a person.
#
# The planning files (looper#13): before the model runs, a missing STATE.md,
# PROPOSALS.md or HISTORY.md is created on main from its template in
# tacplus-dev/roadmap (ROADMAP.md never); after it, a closed issue and each
# release (a new vX.Y.Z tag) get their line at the end of HISTORY.md. A push of
# a commit that touches ROADMAP.md, Jody's, is refused (a pre-push hook in the
# checkout); the issue's own branch is exempt.
#
# Bug or feature (tacplus-dev/roadmap decisions/issue-flow-2026-10-27.md,
# looper#14): an issue labelled neither is classified by the model first (a
# comment and the label). A bug is fixed and released as the next patch, and
# backported. A feature is built only when a ROADMAP.md links it (the
# repository's or tacplus-dev/roadmap's); otherwise the model proposes its
# design (on the issue and in PROPOSALS.md) and the issue is needs-decision.
# The nightly scan files its findings as bugs and never approves a feature;
# every prompt reads the roadmaps before opening an issue.
#
# The think job (looper#28, the pilot on kernel; looper-identity and
# ci/looper.gitlab-ci.yml do the rest): the model and the gates, with no
# identity. It refuses to run beside a write credential or an id_token, reads
# GitHub only with LOOPER_READER_TOKEN (the prepare job's read-only
# tacplus-reader token, which must fail to push), and changes nothing on
# GitHub: the model leaves its change in the checkout and writes its outcome
# (pr, question, blocked) and its comment to DIR; the looper runs the gates on
# that change and writes DIR/patch, DIR/base and DIR/tree, the tested tree.
# The commit job writes it all as <repo>-looper[bot].
#
# Environment: GH_TOKEN (pushes and issue calls), CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN (the
# model), LOOPER_WORK (default /work), LOOPER_RUNS (default LOOPER_WORK/NAME/runs),
# LOOPER_MODEL (default claude-opus-5-5), LOOPER_TIMEOUT (seconds per issue,
# default 14400), LOOPER_STOP_EARLY (default 600), LOOPER_CHECKPOINT_INTERVAL
# (seconds, default 3600), LOOPER_IDLE_MINUTES (default 20), LOOPER_DONE_GRACE
# (seconds, default 300), LOOPER_WATCH_INTERVAL (the watchdog's, seconds,
# default 60), LOOPER_JOB_POLL (seconds, default 300), LOOPER_LAND_MINUTES (a land's
# time before one is recorded), LOOPER_LAND_MARGIN (seconds kept after a land, default 600), LOOPER_GATES (default `just gates` when the
# repository has a justfile), LOOPER_PLAN_TIMEOUT (the plan's model, seconds, default 1200),
# LOOPER_CHILD_CPUS, LOOPER_CHILD_MEMORY_GB, LOOPER_MAX_CHILDREN, LOOPER_JOB_MAX_HOURS (the longest
# --job-timeout, default 11), GIT_AUTHOR_NAME/EMAIL. In a GitLab job, CI_JOB_URL and
# CI_PROJECT_DIR name the job and its artifacts in issue comments, and --list and
# --issue read the project's jobs (CI_API_V4_URL, CI_PROJECT_ID; CI_JOB_ID, this
# job, is not another) with GITLAB_TOKEN, else CI_JOB_TOKEN; a run polls its own.
set -euo pipefail

usage() { sed -n '5,/^set -euo pipefail$/p' "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" | sed '$d' | sed 's/^# \{0,1\}//'; }
case "${1:-}" in -h|--help|help) usage; exit 0 ;; esac

# Run from a private copy: a pull that rewrites this file mid-run must not corrupt the running process.
if [ -z "${LOOPER_RUNNING_COPY:-}" ]; then
  copy="$(mktemp -t repo-looper-run.XXXXXX)"; cp "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" "$copy"
  LOOPER_RUNNING_COPY="$copy" LOOPER_SELF="${LOOPER_SELF:-$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/$(basename "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")}" LOOPER_LIB="${LOOPER_LIB:-$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/looper-lib.sh}" exec bash "$copy" "$@"
fi
# shellcheck source=looper-lib.sh
source "$LOOPER_LIB"
# This script's own path, for the model's --checkpoint.
: "${LOOPER_SELF:=$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/$(basename "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")}"

REPO=""; ISSUE=""; MODE=drain; STEP=""; LAND_GATES=""
while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
  case "$1" in
    --repo) REPO="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --issue) ISSUE="$2"; [[ "$MODE" == drain ]] && MODE=one; shift 2 ;;
    --checkpoint) STEP="$2"; MODE=checkpoint; shift 2 ;;
    --land) MODE=land; shift ;;
    --gates) LAND_GATES="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --child) ISSUE="$2"; MODE=child; shift 2 ;;
    --job-timeout) ISSUE="$2"; MODE=job-timeout; shift 2 ;;
    --scan) MODE=scan; shift ;;
    --list) MODE=list; shift ;;
    --think) THINK_OUT="$2"; MODE=think; shift 2 ;;
    *) die "unknown argument: $1 (see --help)" ;;
  esac
done
[[ -n "$REPO" ]] || die "no repository: --repo NAME (a $LOOPER_ORG repository)"

# The think job holds no identity (looper#28): no write credential, no
# id_token, nothing shaped like a personal or OAuth token. GitHub is read with
# the prepare job's read-only token alone; GitLab's job token goes too.
if [[ "$MODE" == think ]]; then
  [[ -n "$ISSUE" ]] || die "--think needs --issue N"
  held=()
  for v in GH_TOKEN GITHUB_TOKEN GH_ENTERPRISE_TOKEN GITLAB_TOKEN GHCR_PUSH_TOKEN BUILD_TRIGGER_TOKEN \
           TACPLUS_READER_APP_KEY GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS CI_JOB_JWT CI_JOB_JWT_V1 CI_JOB_JWT_V2; do
    [[ -n "${!v:-}" ]] && held+=("$v")
  done
  for v in $(compgen -e); do
    [[ "$v" == *ID_TOKEN && -n "${!v:-}" && " ${held[*]} " != *" $v "* ]] && held+=("$v")
    case "${!v:-}" in ghp_*|github_pat_*|gho_*|ghu_*|glpat-*) [[ " ${held[*]} " == *" $v "* ]] || held+=("$v") ;; esac
  done
  ((${#held[@]} == 0)) || die "the think job holds an identity (${held[*]}): it must hold none"
  [[ -n "${LOOPER_READER_TOKEN:-}" ]] || die "the think job reads GitHub with LOOPER_READER_TOKEN (the prepare job's), and it has none"
  export GH_TOKEN="$LOOPER_READER_TOKEN" LOOPER_THINK=1
  unset LOOPER_READER_TOKEN CI_JOB_TOKEN CI_BUILD_TOKEN CI_REGISTRY_PASSWORD CI_DEPENDENCY_PROXY_PASSWORD CI_REPOSITORY_URL
fi
need gh; need git; need jq

# The GitLab API's auth header: GITLAB_TOKEN, else the job's own token.
gitlab_auth() {
  if [[ -n "${GITLAB_TOKEN:-}" ]]; then echo "PRIVATE-TOKEN: $GITLAB_TOKEN"; else echo "JOB-TOKEN: ${CI_JOB_TOKEN:-}"; fi
}

# The names of the looper jobs that hold a repository (busy() in looper-lib.sh)
# in this GitLab job's project. Outside a GitLab job, none.
project_jobs() {
  [[ -n "${CI_API_V4_URL:-}" && -n "${CI_PROJECT_ID:-}" ]] || return 0
  curl -fsS --retry 2 -H "$(gitlab_auth)" "$CI_API_V4_URL/projects/$CI_PROJECT_ID/jobs?$LOOPER_JOB_SCOPES&per_page=100" \
    | jq -r --arg me "${CI_JOB_ID:-}" '.[] | select((.id | tostring) != $me) | .name'
}

# This GitLab job's status as GitLab reports it (running, failed, canceled...),
# or `refused` when GitLab refuses the job's own token (it does once the job has
# stopped running). Fails when GitLab cannot be read.
job_status() {
  local out code st url="$CI_API_V4_URL/projects/$CI_PROJECT_ID/jobs/$CI_JOB_ID"
  [[ -n "${GITLAB_TOKEN:-}" ]] || url="$CI_API_V4_URL/job"   # what a job token may read: its own job
  out="$(mktemp)"
  code="$(curl -sS --retry 2 -H "$(gitlab_auth)" -o "$out" -w '%{http_code}' "$url")" || code=""
  st="$(jq -r '.status // empty' "$out" 2>/dev/null)" || st=""
  rm -f "$out"
  case "$code" in
    200) [[ -n "$st" ]] && echo "$st" ;;
    401|403) [[ -z "${GITLAB_TOKEN:-}" ]] && echo refused ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
}

# A run killed outright (its runner gone, looper#20) leaves its issue
# looper:in-progress, and no run holds it. The job for that issue takes it as a
# resume when it is otherwise ready and no other looper job of the repository
# (this job excepted) is created, pending, waiting or running: the label is
# this job's. Outside a GitLab job, or when GitLab cannot be read, never.
orphaned() { # issue
  local l jobs
  [[ -n "${CI_API_V4_URL:-}" && -n "${CI_PROJECT_ID:-}" ]] || return 1
  l="$(gh issue view "$1" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --json state,labels --jq 'select(.state == "OPEN") | .labels[].name')" || return 1
  grep -qx "$LABEL_IN_PROGRESS" <<<"$l" || return 1
  if grep -qxE "$LABEL_STUCK|$LABEL_DECISION" <<<"$l"; then return 1; fi
  ready "$REPO" "$1" || return 1
  jobs="$(project_jobs)" || return 1
  ! busy "$REPO" "$jobs" >/dev/null
}

# The plan (looper#16): a repository whose looper job is already created,
# pending, waiting for its resource_group or running gets no other one; that
# job takes the next ready issue when it runs. When GitLab cannot be read,
# the repository is planned as before. The log is stderr: stdout is the list.
if [[ "$MODE" == list ]]; then
  if jobs="$(project_jobs)"; then
    held="$(busy "$REPO" "$jobs" | tr '\n' ' ')" || true
    [[ -z "$held" ]] || { log "$REPO already has a looper job: ${held% }; not planned again" >&2; exit 0; }
  else
    log "could not read this project's jobs; planning $REPO anyway" >&2
  fi
  approved_issues "$REPO" | while read -r n; do if ready "$REPO" "$n"; then echo "$n"; fi; done
  exit 0
fi
[[ "$MODE" != checkpoint || -n "$ISSUE" ]] || die "--checkpoint needs --issue N"
[[ "$MODE" != land || -n "$ISSUE" ]] || die "--land needs --issue N"

WORK="${LOOPER_WORK:-/work}/$REPO"
# The repository's checkout, the parent's; each child works in its own
# worktree of it, on its own branch (looper#32). BRANCH is the checkout's.
MAIN_CHECKOUT="$WORK/checkout"
CHECKOUT="$MAIN_CHECKOUT"; BRANCH=main
child_dir() { echo "$WORK/worktrees/$1"; } # issue
# A child, and the model's --checkpoint and --land from a child's worktree.
if [[ "$MODE" == child ]] || [[ "$MODE" =~ ^(checkpoint|land)$ && -e "$(child_dir "$ISSUE")/.git" ]]; then
  CHECKOUT="$(child_dir "$ISSUE")"; BRANCH="looper/$REPO-$ISSUE"
fi
MODEL="${LOOPER_MODEL:-claude-opus-5-5}"
TIMEOUT="${LOOPER_TIMEOUT:-14400}"
MAX_LIMIT=36000   # 10h: what a `Timeout:` line may ask for
JOB_MAX_HOURS="${LOOPER_JOB_MAX_HOURS:-11}"   # the longest job --job-timeout asks GitLab for
PLAN_TIMEOUT="${LOOPER_PLAN_TIMEOUT:-1200}"
STOP_EARLY="${LOOPER_STOP_EARLY:-600}"
CHECKPOINT_INTERVAL="${LOOPER_CHECKPOINT_INTERVAL:-3600}"
IDLE="$(awk -v m="${LOOPER_IDLE_MINUTES:-20}" 'BEGIN { printf "%d", m * 60 }')"
DONE_GRACE="${LOOPER_DONE_GRACE:-300}"
WATCH_INTERVAL="${LOOPER_WATCH_INTERVAL:-60}"
JOB_POLL="${LOOPER_JOB_POLL:-300}"
RECORD_LINES=400   # the claude.log tail a checkpoint keeps under .looper/
RUNS="${LOOPER_RUNS:-$WORK/runs}"

# The run's limit in seconds: a line `Timeout: <hours>h` in the issue's body,
# at most 10h, else LOOPER_TIMEOUT.
run_limit() { # issue
  local body h
  body="$(gh issue view "$1" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --json body --jq .body)" || { echo "$TIMEOUT"; return 0; }
  h="$(grep -ioE '^[[:space:]]*timeout:[[:space:]]*[0-9]{1,3}h[[:space:]]*$' <<<"$body" | head -n 1 | tr -dc 0-9)" || true
  if [[ -z "$h" || "$((10#$h))" == 0 ]]; then echo "$TIMEOUT"
  elif (( 10#$h * 3600 > MAX_LIMIT )); then echo "$MAX_LIMIT"
  else echo "$((10#$h * 3600))"; fi
}
hours() { # seconds, as 6h, 4h37m or 12m (minutes rounded up)
  local m=$(( ($1 + 59) / 60 ))
  if (( m < 60 )); then echo "${m}m"; elif (( m % 60 )); then echo "$(( m / 60 ))h$(( m % 60 ))m"; else echo "$(( m / 60 ))h"; fi
}

# In a GitLab job, the parent's deadline (epoch seconds): the job's end, from
# its start (CI_JOB_STARTED_AT, else now) and CI_JOB_TIMEOUT, less the hour it
# keeps for the checkpoints, the outcomes and the lands. Empty outside one.
JOB_MARGIN=3600
job_end() {
  local t0
  [[ "${CI_JOB_TIMEOUT:-}" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 0
  t0="$(date -d "${CI_JOB_STARTED_AT:-now}" +%s 2>/dev/null || date +%s)"
  echo $(( t0 + CI_JOB_TIMEOUT ))
}
job_deadline() {
  local end; end="$(job_end)"
  [[ -z "$end" ]] || echo $(( end - JOB_MARGIN ))
}

# What a land is expected to take (looper#38), in seconds: the repository's
# last land that passed its gates (LAND_TIME, kept with the checkout), else
# LOOPER_LAND_MINUTES, else 70 minutes for tacplus (its full gates) and 30 for
# the others. A land starts only when that, and LAND_MARGIN after it for its
# outcome, fit in the job's time left.
LAND_TIME="$WORK/land-seconds"
LAND_MARGIN="${LOOPER_LAND_MARGIN:-600}"
land_estimate() {
  local s; s="$(cat "$LAND_TIME" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  if [[ "$s" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then echo "$s"
  elif [[ "${LOOPER_LAND_MINUTES:-}" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then echo $(( LOOPER_LAND_MINUTES * 60 ))
  elif [[ "$REPO" == tacplus ]]; then echo 4200
  else echo 1800; fi
}

# The CI job's timeout, sized for the plan, not only its issue (looper#33):
# the longest limit of the issue and the ready issues the parent could start
# beside it (up to LOOPER_MAX_CHILDREN in all), plus the plan's model's time
# before they start and an hour for the checkout, the checkpoints and the
# outcomes, rounded up to hours and at most JOB_MAX_HOURS. A job sized for its
# issue alone left no room for any other child (the parent's deadline is the
# job's end less that hour).
job_timeout() { # issue
  local n limit longest k=1 max="${LOOPER_MAX_CHILDREN:-6}" h
  longest="$(run_limit "$1")"
  for n in $(approved_issues "$REPO"); do
    (( max <= 0 || k < max )) || break
    [[ "$n" == "$1" ]] && continue
    ready "$REPO" "$n" || continue
    k=$(( k + 1 ))
    limit="$(run_limit "$n")"; (( limit <= longest )) || longest="$limit"
  done
  h=$(( (longest + PLAN_TIMEOUT + 3599) / 3600 + 1 ))
  (( h <= JOB_MAX_HOURS )) || h="$JOB_MAX_HOURS"
  echo "${h}h"
}
[[ "$MODE" == job-timeout ]] && { job_timeout "$ISSUE"; exit 0; }
need claude
mkdir -p "$RUNS"
gh auth setup-git >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

# git fetch from origin in a checkout or worktree, tried again a few times:
# the children's worktrees share the checkout's refs, and two fetches at once
# can fail to lock one.
fetch() { # dir [refspec...]
  local d="$1" i; shift
  for i in 1 2 3 4 5; do
    git -C "$d" fetch -q origin "$@" && return 0
    sleep "$i"
  done
  return 1
}

# The repository's checkout at origin/main. Anything a previous run left
# unpushed is kept on a local branch for a person to look at, never pushed.
sync_checkout() {
  if [[ ! -d "$CHECKOUT/.git" ]]; then
    gh repo clone "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" "$CHECKOUT" -- --quiet
  fi
  fetch "$CHECKOUT"
  # Keep what a previous run left before moving: a cancelled or killed run can
  # leave the checkout dirty, detached, or on main with local commits, and then
  # switching to main fails (the reused-worker case, tacplus#161 2026-10-27).
  local ahead_main=0
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" rev-parse -q --verify refs/heads/main >/dev/null &&
    ahead_main="$(git -C "$CHECKOUT" rev-list --count origin/main..main)"
  if [[ -n "$(git -C "$CHECKOUT" status --porcelain)" || \
        "$(git -C "$CHECKOUT" rev-list --count origin/main..HEAD)" != 0 || "$ahead_main" != 0 ]]; then
    local keep; keep="looper/left-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)"
    git -C "$CHECKOUT" add -A && git -C "$CHECKOUT" commit -q -m "left by an unfinished looper run" || true
    git -C "$CHECKOUT" branch -q "$keep"
    [[ "$ahead_main" != 0 && "$(git -C "$CHECKOUT" symbolic-ref -q --short HEAD)" != main ]] &&
      git -C "$CHECKOUT" branch -q "$keep-main" main
    log "kept unpushed work on local branch $keep"
  fi
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" checkout -q -f -B main origin/main
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" reset -q --hard origin/main
  guard_roadmap
}

# A child's worktree (looper#32), made by the parent before the child starts:
# a worktree of the checkout at origin/main, on the issue's own branch. One a
# killed run left is replaced, anything unpushed in it kept on a local branch
# first, as sync_checkout does.
prepare_worktree() { # issue
  local wt b keep; wt="$(child_dir "$1")"; b="looper/$REPO-$1"
  if [[ -e "$wt/.git" ]]; then
    if [[ -n "$(git -C "$wt" status --porcelain 2>/dev/null)" || "$(git -C "$wt" rev-list --count origin/main..HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo 0)" != 0 ]]; then
      keep="looper/left-$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)-$1"
      git -C "$wt" add -A && git -C "$wt" commit -q --no-verify -m "left by an unfinished looper run" || true
      git -C "$wt" branch -q "$keep" && log "kept unpushed work of #$1 on local branch $keep"
    fi
  fi
  git -C "$MAIN_CHECKOUT" worktree remove -f "$wt" >/dev/null 2>&1 || rm -rf "$wt"
  git -C "$MAIN_CHECKOUT" worktree prune
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$wt")"
  git -C "$MAIN_CHECKOUT" worktree add -q -f -B "$b" "$wt" origin/main
}

# The planning files every repository carries (tacplus-dev/roadmap
# decisions/repo-roadmap-files-2026-10-27.md, looper#13). ROADMAP.md is Jody's:
# an agent changes it at his request, a looper never does. The model keeps
# STATE.md and PROPOSALS.md. HISTORY.md is append-only, and this script appends
# its line for each closed issue and each release. A missing one is created
# from its template in tacplus-dev/roadmap, ROADMAP.md never.
PLANNING_FILES=(STATE.md PROPOSALS.md HISTORY.md)
TEMPLATES="roadmap/contents/templates/repo"   # a repository of LOOPER_ORG and its path

# A planning file's template, with the repository's name filled in.
template() { # file
  local t; t="$(mktemp)"
  gh api "repos/$LOOPER_ORG/$TEMPLATES/$1" -H "Accept: application/vnd.github.raw" > "$t" || { rm -f "$t"; return 1; }
  sed "s/<repo>/$REPO/g" "$t"; rm -f "$t"
}

# Refuses, in the checkout's pre-push hook, any push of a commit that touches
# ROADMAP.md: the model's pushes and this script's alike. The issue's own
# branch (looper/*) is exempt, so a checkpoint still keeps all the work.
# Commits already on origin (an agent's, at Jody's request) are not this push's.
# The hook is the checkout's and every worktree's (the common git dir). From a
# worktree it also refuses a push to main that is not a landing (looper#32):
# --land, or this script's own commits, under the landing lock.
guard_roadmap() {
  local hook; hook="$(cd "$CHECKOUT" && cd "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)" && pwd)/hooks/pre-push"
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$hook")"
  cat > "$hook" <<'HOOK'
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Installed by repo-looper (looper#13): a looper never pushes a commit that touches ROADMAP.md.
z=0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
worktree=""; [[ "$(git rev-parse --absolute-git-dir)" != "$(cd "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)" && pwd)" ]] && worktree=1
while read -r _ lsha rref rsha; do
  [[ "$lsha" == "$z" || "$rref" == refs/heads/looper/* ]] && continue
  # Not the issues' branches (looper/*): a checkpoint carries a commit there before it lands (looper#38).
  not=(--exclude="$1/looper/*" --remotes="$1"); [[ "$rsha" != "$z" ]] && git cat-file -e "$rsha" 2>/dev/null && not+=("$rsha")
  c="$(git rev-list -n 1 "$lsha" --not "${not[@]}" -- ROADMAP.md)"
  if [[ -n "$c" ]]; then
    echo "repo-looper: refused to push $rref: commit $(git rev-parse --short "$c") touches ROADMAP.md, which only Jody changes (through an agent). Propose it in PROPOSALS.md instead." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  # Installed by repo-looper (looper#32): a child lands on main only through --land.
  if [[ -n "$worktree" && "$rref" == refs/heads/main && -z "${LOOPER_LANDING:-}" ]]; then
    echo "repo-looper: refused to push to main from this worktree: land it with repo-looper --land, which rebases it on main and re-runs the gates under the repository's landing lock." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
done
exit 0
HOOK
  chmod +x "$hook"
}

# Everything that pushes to main holds the repository's landing lock
# (looper#32): the children of one parent land one at a time.
LAND_LOCK="$WORK/.land.lock"

# Commits a change to main and pushes it, from a scratch worktree at
# origin/main: the checkout is untouched. The change is a command run in that
# worktree; when the push is rejected it is made again on the new origin/main.
# Holds the landing lock. Never fatal.
land() { # message command [args...]
  local msg="$1" wt; shift
  wt="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$WORK"
  ( flock 8
    for i in 1 2 3; do
      fetch "$MAIN_CHECKOUT" main || break
      if (( i == 1 )); then git -C "$MAIN_CHECKOUT" worktree add -q -f --detach "$wt" origin/main || break
      else git -C "$wt" checkout -q -f --detach origin/main && git -C "$wt" clean -fdq; fi
      ( cd "$wt" && "$@" ) || break
      [[ -n "$(git -C "$wt" status --porcelain)" ]] || break
      git -C "$wt" add -A && git -C "$wt" commit -q -m "$msg" || break
      if LOOPER_LANDING=1 git -C "$wt" push -q origin HEAD:refs/heads/main; then log "pushed to main: $msg"; break; fi
      (( i < 3 )) || log "could not push to main: $msg"
    done
  ) 8>"$LAND_LOCK" || true
  git -C "$MAIN_CHECKOUT" worktree remove -f "$wt" 2>/dev/null || rm -rf "$wt"
  git -C "$MAIN_CHECKOUT" worktree prune
}

# The gates a landing re-runs: --gates, else LOOPER_GATES, else `just gates`
# when the repository has a justfile; nothing when none is known.
land_gates() {
  if [[ -n "$LAND_GATES" ]]; then echo "$LAND_GATES"
  elif [[ -n "${LOOPER_GATES:-}" ]]; then echo "$LOOPER_GATES"
  elif [[ -f "$CHECKOUT/justfile" ]]; then echo "just gates"; fi
}

# --land, the model's step (looper#32): lands the commits of the child's branch
# on main. Under the repository's landing lock it rebases them onto the
# current main, re-runs the gates on the result and pushes; a rejected push is
# rebased and tried again. Lands nothing, and says why, when the change is not
# committed, the rebase conflicts (the model rebases, resolves and lands
# again), the gates fail or change a tracked file. Each landing is recorded in
# the run's `landed` file, "<main before> <main after>".
land_work() {
  local gates out; gates="$(land_gates)"
  [[ -n "$gates" ]] || die "no gates to re-run before landing: $REPO has no justfile and LOOPER_GATES is unset; pass the command you verified with as --gates CMD"
  cd "$CHECKOUT"
  [[ -z "$(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no)" ]] || die "uncommitted changes: commit your change on $BRANCH first; --land lands its commits"
  out="${LOOPER_RUN:-$(mktemp -d)}"; mkdir -p "$WORK"
  ( flock 8
    for i in 1 2 3; do
      fetch . main || { log "could not fetch main; nothing landed"; exit 1; }
      [[ "$(git rev-list --count origin/main..HEAD)" != 0 ]] || { log "nothing to land: $BRANCH has no commit that main lacks"; exit 0; }
      before="$(git rev-parse origin/main)"
      if ! git rebase -q origin/main >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        conflicts="$(git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U | paste -sd' ' -)"
        git rebase --abort >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
        log "nothing landed: rebasing $BRANCH onto main conflicts in: $conflicts. Rebase it yourself (git fetch origin && git rebase origin/main), resolve them, verify, and land again." >&2
        exit 3
      fi
      log "landing $BRANCH: the gates ($gates) on $(git rev-parse --short HEAD), rebased on main $(git rev-parse --short origin/main)"
      grc=0; t=$SECONDS; bash -c "$gates" > "$out/gates.log" 2>&1 || grc=$?
      (( grc != 0 )) || echo $(( SECONDS - t )) > "$LAND_TIME"
      if (( grc != 0 )); then
        { log "nothing landed: the gates failed (exit $grc), on your commits rebased on main; their end:"; tail -n 30 "$out/gates.log"; } >&2
        exit 4
      fi
      if [[ -n "$(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no)" ]]; then
        log "nothing landed: the gates changed tracked files ($(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no | awk '{ print $2 }' | paste -sd' ' -)): it is not the tested tree" >&2
        exit 4
      fi
      if LOOPER_LANDING=1 git push -q origin HEAD:refs/heads/main; then
        echo "$before $(git rev-parse HEAD)" >> "$out/landed"
        log "landed on main: $(git rev-parse --short "$before")..$(git rev-parse --short HEAD)"
        exit 0
      fi
      log "the push to main was rejected (main moved); rebasing again"
    done
    log "nothing landed: main kept moving; land again" >&2
    exit 5
  ) 8>"$LAND_LOCK"
}

# The parent owns its children's lands (looper#34). A land waits for the lock
# and runs the full gates, which took over two hours with three children on
# one runner: run inside the model, it was lost with everything queued behind
# it when the child's limit fired. So --land from a child (LOOPER_LANDER, the
# parent's queue) only queues it: the parent's lander runs the queued lands one
# at a time, in its own process, and --land waits for the result and prints it.
# A land still queued or running when its child ends is landed all the same,
# under the parent's job timeout; the lander says on the issue what came of it,
# and the issue stays approved for the next run to finish (comment, close).
# The queue, LANDS, per issue: N.req (queued), N.running, then N.ran (the
# request), N.out, N.err and N.rc (the result, written last); N.orphan when the
# child ended first, and N.claimed by whichever reads the result.
LANDS="$WORK/lands"
land_pending() { [[ -n "${LOOPER_LANDER:-}" && ( -e "$LANDS/$1.req" || -e "$LANDS/$1.running" ) ]]; } # issue

# A land the lander does not start because it would not finish before the job
# ends (looper#38): it stays queued on the issue's branch for the next job.
# The run's `land` file (gates=, state=queued or deferred) rides on its
# checkpoints as .looper/land while the land has no outcome, so a land the
# job's end cut off is still seen by the next run, which lands it first.
LAND_DEFERRED=75

# --land from a child: queues the land with the parent, its branch marked
# land-pending, and waits for it.
land_request() {
  local gates n="$ISSUE" rc=0; gates="$(land_gates)"
  [[ -n "$gates" ]] || die "no gates to re-run before landing: $REPO has no justfile and LOOPER_GATES is unset; pass the command you verified with as --gates CMD"
  cd "$CHECKOUT"
  [[ -z "$(git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no)" ]] || die "uncommitted changes: commit your change on $BRANCH first; --land lands its commits"
  if [[ -n "${LOOPER_RUN:-}" && ! -e "$LANDS/$n.rc" ]] && ! land_pending "$n"; then
    printf 'gates=%s\nstate=queued\n' "$gates" > "$LOOPER_RUN/land"
    checkpoint "$n" "land queued" "$LOOPER_RUN"
  fi
  land_wait "$gates" || rc=$?
  [[ -z "${LOOPER_RUN:-}" ]] || (( rc == LAND_DEFERRED )) || rm -f "$LOOPER_RUN/land"
  [[ -z "${LOOPER_RUN:-}" ]] || checkpoint "$n" "land $( (( rc == LAND_DEFERRED )) && echo deferred || echo done)" "$LOOPER_RUN"
  return "$rc"
}

# Queues the checkout's land with the parent and waits for it, printing what
# came of it. When the parent's lander is gone and has not taken the request,
# lands it here.
land_wait() { # gates
  local gates="$1" n="$ISSUE" rc
  if [[ -e "$LANDS/$n.rc" ]]; then log "a land of $BRANCH ended unread; its result:"
  elif land_pending "$n"; then log "a land of $BRANCH is already queued; waiting for it"
  else
    printf 'run=%s\ngates=%s\n' "${LOOPER_RUN:-}" "$gates" > "$LANDS/$n.req.new" && mv "$LANDS/$n.req.new" "$LANDS/$n.req"
    log "queued: the looper lands $BRANCH after the lands queued before it, one at a time; waiting for it. If your time runs out first, it still lands it after you and says so on the issue."
  fi
  until [[ -e "$LANDS/$n.rc" ]]; do
    if ! kill -0 "${LOOPER_LANDER_PID:-0}" 2>/dev/null && mv "$LANDS/$n.req" "$LANDS/$n.taken" 2>/dev/null; then
      rm -f "$LANDS/$n.taken"; log "the looper's lander is gone; landing here"; land_work; return
    fi
    sleep 1
  done
  { [[ ! -e "$LANDS/$n.orphan" ]] && mkdir "$LANDS/$n.claimed" 2>/dev/null; } || die "this run has ended: the looper reports the land on the issue"
  rc="$(cat "$LANDS/$n.rc")"; cat "$LANDS/$n.out"; cat "$LANDS/$n.err" >&2
  rm -rf "$LANDS/$n".{ran,out,err,rc,claimed}
  return "$rc"
}

# The parent's lander: the queued lands, oldest first, one at a time, each in
# its child's worktree, until the parent says it is done (LANDS/.done: the
# queue drained first) or must stop (LANDS/.stop: after the land under way).
lander() {
  local f n run gates rc
  while :; do
    [[ -e "$LANDS/.stop" ]] && return 0
    f="$(ls -tr "$LANDS"/*.req 2>/dev/null | head -n 1)" || true
    if [[ -z "$f" ]]; then [[ -e "$LANDS/.done" ]] && return 0; sleep 1; continue; fi
    n="$(basename "$f" .req)"
    mv "$f" "$LANDS/$n.running" 2>/dev/null || continue
    run="$(sed -n 's/^run=//p' "$LANDS/$n.running")"; gates="$(sed -n 's/^gates=//p' "$LANDS/$n.running")"
    rc=0
    if ! land_fits; then
      log "#$n: deferred to the next job: the job ends in $(hours "$(( $(job_end) - $(date +%s) ))"), a land takes about $(hours "$(land_estimate)")"
      land_defer "$n" "$run" > "$LANDS/$n.out" 2> "$LANDS/$n.err"; rc=$LAND_DEFERRED
    else
      log "landing #$n ($(wip_branch "$n"))"
      ( CHECKOUT="$(child_dir "$n")"; BRANCH="$(wip_branch "$n")"; LAND_GATES="$gates"; LOOPER_RUN="$run"; land_work ) \
        > "$LANDS/$n.out" 2> "$LANDS/$n.err" || rc=$?
      if (( rc == 0 )); then log "#$n: $(tail -n 1 "$LANDS/$n.out" | cut -c24-)"; else log "#$n: nothing landed (exit $rc)"; fi
      # It has an outcome now: the branch is no longer land-pending.
      [[ -z "$run" ]] || rm -f "$run/land"
    fi
    mv "$LANDS/$n.running" "$LANDS/$n.ran"
    echo "$rc" > "$LANDS/$n.rc.new" && mv "$LANDS/$n.rc.new" "$LANDS/$n.rc"
    if [[ -e "$LANDS/$n.orphan" ]] && mkdir "$LANDS/$n.claimed" 2>/dev/null; then orphan_land "$n"; fi
  done
}

# A land fits when its expected time and LAND_MARGIN fit in the job's time
# left; outside a GitLab job every land fits.
land_fits() {
  local end; end="$(job_end)"
  [[ -z "$end" ]] || (( $(land_estimate) <= end - $(date +%s) - LAND_MARGIN ))
}

# A land that does not fit (looper#38): not started. Its branch stays
# land-pending (state=deferred) for the next job, which lands it first, and the
# issue says so and stays approved. Prints, for --land, what came of it.
land_defer() { # issue run-dir
  local n="$1" run="$2" end est left why
  end="$(job_end)"; est="$(land_estimate)"; left=$(( end - $(date +%s) )); (( left > 0 )) || left=0
  why="land deferred to the next job: not enough time left in this one (${CI_JOB_URL:-$CI_JOB_ID}): it ends in $(hours "$left"), and a land of $REPO takes about $(hours "$est"), with $(hours "$LAND_MARGIN") kept after it for its outcome"
  [[ -z "$run" || ! -f "$run/land" ]] || sed -i 's/^state=.*/state=deferred/' "$run/land"
  gh issue comment "$n" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --body "The looper's $why. Its commits stay queued on [\`$(wip_branch "$n")\`]($(wip_url "$n")): the next run lands them first, then finishes the issue. This issue stays $LABEL_APPROVED." >/dev/null || true
  log "nothing landed: $why. Do not land again in this run: checkpoint and end it; the next run lands it first." >&2
}

# The result of a land whose child ended first, on its issue: landed (and the
# branch checkpointed again from the worktree, now on main), or why not. The
# issue stays approved either way: the next run resumes from the branch. A
# deferred land was told on the issue already.
orphan_land() { # issue
  local n="$1" rc run b what left="lands it again"
  rc="$(cat "$LANDS/$n.rc")"; run="$(sed -n 's/^run=//p' "$LANDS/$n.ran")"; b="$(wip_branch "$n")"
  if [[ "$rc" == "$LAND_DEFERRED" ]]; then
    ( CHECKOUT="$(child_dir "$n")"; checkpoint "$n" "land deferred to the next job" "$run" )
    rm -rf "$LANDS/$n".{ran,out,err,rc,orphan,claimed}; return 0
  fi
  ( CHECKOUT="$(child_dir "$n")"; checkpoint "$n" "landed by the looper after the run" "$run" )
  if [[ "$rc" == 0 ]]; then
    what="The looper landed this issue's commits on main after its run ended: they were queued to land when the run stopped."
    [[ -n "$run" && -s "$run/landed" ]] && what+=" Main: $(tail -n 1 "$run/landed" | awk '{ print substr($1, 1, 7) ".." substr($2, 1, 7) }')."
    left="finishes it (comment, any release, close)"
  else
    what="The looper could not land the commits this issue's run queued before it stopped (exit $rc):

$(printf '~~~~~~\n%s\n~~~~~~' "$(tail -n 30 "$LANDS/$n.err" | redact)")"
  fi
  gh issue comment "$n" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --body "$what

This issue stays $LABEL_APPROVED: the next run resumes from [\`$b\`]($(wip_url "$n")) and $left.${run:+ Run record: $(run_record "$run").}" >/dev/null || true
  rm -rf "$LANDS/$n".{ran,out,err,rc,orphan,claimed}
}

# A branch an earlier job left land-pending (looper#38): its land was
# deferred, or the job's end cut it off with no outcome said. Before the model
# runs, the run lands what was queued (the parent of the branch's last
# checkpoint: the commits as they were) and says on the issue what came of it.
# Deferred again, the branch stays land-pending for the next job, and it
# returns LAND_DEFERRED; otherwise the checkout is main again, for resume.
land_prior() { # issue run-dir
  local n="$1" run="$2" b m gates state rc=0 cut="" what
  b="$(wip_branch "$n")"
  wip_on_origin "$n" || return 0
  fetch "$CHECKOUT" "+refs/heads/$b:refs/remotes/origin/$b"
  m="$(git -C "$CHECKOUT" show "origin/$b:.looper/land" 2>/dev/null)" || return 0
  gates="$(sed -n 's/^gates=//p' <<<"$m")"; state="$(sed -n 's/^state=//p' <<<"$m")"
  [[ "$state" == deferred ]] || cut="The land this issue's run queued in an earlier job was cut off: that job ended while it was queued or running, and nothing here said what came of it. "
  log "#$n: $b is land-pending (${state:-queued} by an earlier job); landing it first"
  if ! git -C "$CHECKOUT" checkout -q -f -B "$BRANCH" "origin/$b^" || ! git -C "$CHECKOUT" clean -fdq; then
    log "#$n: cannot check out what $b queued; the run resumes from it instead"
    git -C "$CHECKOUT" checkout -q -f -B "$BRANCH" origin/main || true; return 0
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$m" > "$run/land"
  ( LAND_GATES="$gates"; LOOPER_RUN="$run"
    if [[ -n "${LOOPER_LANDER:-}" && -d "$LOOPER_LANDER" ]]; then land_wait "$gates"; else land_work; fi ) > "$run/prior-land" 2>&1 || rc=$?
  if (( rc == LAND_DEFERRED )); then
    # The branch as it was, but for its land's state.
    git -C "$CHECKOUT" read-tree -u --reset "origin/$b"
    checkpoint "$n" "land deferred to the next job" "$run"
    [[ -z "$cut" ]] || gh issue comment "$n" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --body "${cut}This job has no time left to land it either: it stays queued for the next." >/dev/null || true
    return "$LAND_DEFERRED"
  fi
  rm -f "$run/land"
  if (( rc == 0 )); then
    what="${cut}The looper landed it first, before this run's model:"
    if [[ -s "$run/landed" ]]; then what+=" main $(tail -n 1 "$run/landed" | awk '{ print substr($1, 1, 7) ".." substr($2, 1, 7) }')."
    else what+=" its commits were on main already."; fi
  else
    what="${cut}The looper tried it first, before this run's model, and nothing landed (exit $rc):

$(printf '~~~~~~\n%s\n~~~~~~' "$(tail -n 30 "$run/prior-land" | redact)")

The run goes on from the branch."
  fi
  [[ "$state" == deferred && "$rc" == 0 ]] && what="The land deferred from an earlier job: the looper landed it first, before this run's model:${what#*model:}"
  gh issue comment "$n" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --body "$what" >/dev/null || true
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" checkout -q -f -B "$BRANCH" origin/main && git -C "$CHECKOUT" clean -fdq
  return 0
}

add_planning_files() {
  local f
  for f in "${PLANNING_FILES[@]}"; do
    [[ -e "$f" ]] || template "$f" > "$f" || { rm -f "$f"; log "no template for $f"; }
  done
}

append_history() { # line...
  [[ -e HISTORY.md ]] || template HISTORY.md > HISTORY.md || : > HISTORY.md
  [[ -z "$(tail -c 1 HISTORY.md)" ]] || echo >> HISTORY.md
  printf '%s\n' "$@" >> HISTORY.md
}

# The release tags (vX.Y.Z) on origin.
release_tags() {
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" ls-remote --tags --refs origin 'refs/tags/v*' | sed 's#.*refs/tags/##' \
    | grep -E '^v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$' | sort -V || true
}

gates_hint() {
  if [[ -n "${LOOPER_GATES:-}" ]]; then echo "$LOOPER_GATES"
  elif [[ -f "$CHECKOUT/justfile" ]]; then echo "just gates (see the justfile)"
  else echo "the repository's own build and tests (README, CONTRIBUTING, CI workflows)"; fi
}

# claude's stream-json events as the lines of claude.log: its text, each tool
# call and the start of each result. A line that is not an event (an error on
# stderr) is kept as it is.
RENDER='
  def short: tostring | gsub("\n"; " ") | if length > 300 then .[0:300] + "..." else . end;
  . as $line | try (
    fromjson
    | if type != "object" then $line
      elif .type == "assistant" then .message.content[]
        | if .type == "text" then .text
          elif .type == "tool_use" then "> \(.name) \(.input | .command // .file_path // .pattern // .url // .description // . | short)"
          else empty end
      elif .type == "user" then .message.content[]? | select(type == "object" and .type == "tool_result")
        | "< " + (.content | if type == "array" then map(.text? // "") | join(" ") else . end | short)
      elif .type == "result" then "= \(.subtype // "result")\(if .result then ": " + .result else "" end)"
      elif .type == "system" then "= \(.subtype // "system")"
      else empty end
  ) catch $line'

# What the model never holds (looper#25): the job's GitLab credentials (its
# GITLAB_TOKEN, the job token and the variables that carry it, any id_token),
# which the looper keeps for its own reads of GitLab. It runs as the job's user,
# so this is not a boundary: the tokens' reach is (README, Credentials).
MODEL_WITHHELD=(GITLAB_TOKEN LOOPER_TRIGGER_TOKEN SEABEAM_READ_TOKEN CI_JOB_TOKEN CI_BUILD_TOKEN CI_REGISTRY_PASSWORD
                CI_DEPENDENCY_PROXY_PASSWORD CI_REPOSITORY_URL CI_JOB_JWT CI_JOB_JWT_V1 CI_JOB_JWT_V2)
model_env() { # the env(1) arguments that withhold them
  local v
  for v in $(compgen -e); do
    if [[ " ${MODEL_WITHHELD[*]} " == *" $v "* || "$v" == *ID_TOKEN ]]; then printf '%s\n' -u "$v"; fi
  done
}

# Runs the model with the watchdog beside it. A run dir with an issue is an
# issue's run: the model gets LOOPER_RUN (for its --checkpoint) and the
# watchdog also ends it once the issue is done.
run_claude() { # prompt-file log-file seconds run-dir [issue]
  local rc=0 dir pid fmt watcher k withheld
  mapfile -t withheld < <(model_env)
  dir="$(mktemp -d)"; mkfifo "$dir/out"
  jq -R -r --unbuffered "$RENDER" < "$dir/out" > "$2" & fmt=$!
  ( cd "$CHECKOUT" && LOOPER_RUN="${5:+$4}" exec env "${withheld[@]}" timeout -k 30 "$3" claude --print --verbose --output-format stream-json \
      --model "$MODEL" --permission-mode acceptEdits \
      --allowedTools "Bash,Read,Edit,Write,Grep,Glob,Task,Agent,TodoWrite,WebFetch,WebSearch" \
      < "$1" > "$dir/out" 2>&1 ) & pid=$!
  watch_run "$pid" "$2" "$4" "${5:-}" & watcher=$!
  wait "$pid" || rc=$?
  kill "$watcher" 2>/dev/null; wait "$watcher" 2>/dev/null || true
  # The log is complete when the model's output closes; a process the model
  # left holding that output does not hold the run.
  ( sleep 10; kill "$fmt" 2>/dev/null ) & k=$!
  wait "$fmt" 2>/dev/null || true
  kill "$k" 2>/dev/null; wait "$k" 2>/dev/null || true
  rm -rf "$dir"
  return "$rc"
}

# The issue's run is done: the issue is closed, or the run wrote its
# Blocked-by outcome (its last step, whether it pushed or not: looper#22).
run_done() { # issue run-dir
  if [[ "$MODE" == think ]]; then grep -qE '^type:[[:space:]]*[a-z]+' "$THINK_OUT/outcome" 2>/dev/null; return; fi
  if [[ -f "$2/outcome" ]] && grep -qi '^[[:space:]]*blocked-by:' "$2/outcome"; then return 0; fi
  [[ "$(gh issue view "$1" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --json state --jq .state 2>/dev/null)" == CLOSED ]]
}

# The watchdog, every LOOPER_WATCH_INTERVAL until killed: stops the model when
# it went idle (no output, nothing changed in the checkout and no land of its
# queued or running for LOOPER_IDLE_MINUTES), when its issue's run is done and it has not exited
# LOOPER_DONE_GRACE seconds later, or, polling every LOOPER_JOB_POLL in a
# GitLab job, when GitLab no longer runs the issue run's job (looper#21): it
# reports another status, or refuses the job's token after accepting it. An
# answer that is no status (GitLab down) stops nothing. Writes why to the run's
# `stopped` file.
watch_run() { # pid log run-dir [issue]
  local pid="$1" run="$3" n="${4:-}" size=-1 s now done_at="" z="" polled st accepted=""
  trap 'kill "$z" 2>/dev/null; exit 0' TERM
  touch "$run/.active"; polled="$(date +%s)"
  while :; do
    sleep "$WATCH_INTERVAL" & z=$!; wait "$z"
    now="$(date +%s)"; s="$(stat -c %s "$2" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
    if [[ "$s" != "$size" || -n "$(find "$CHECKOUT" -newer "$run/.active" -print -quit 2>/dev/null)" ]] || { [[ -n "$n" ]] && land_pending "$n"; }; then
      size="$s"; touch "$run/.active"
    elif (( now - $(stat -c %Y "$run/.active") >= IDLE )); then
      echo idle > "$run/stopped"; kill -TERM "$pid" 2>/dev/null; exit 0
    fi
    if [[ -n "$n" ]] && run_done "$n" "$run"; then
      done_at="${done_at:-$now}"
      if (( now - done_at >= DONE_GRACE )); then echo "done" > "$run/stopped"; kill -TERM "$pid" 2>/dev/null; exit 0; fi
    fi
    if [[ -n "$n" && -n "${CI_API_V4_URL:-}" && -n "${CI_PROJECT_ID:-}" && -n "${CI_JOB_ID:-}" ]] && (( now - polled >= JOB_POLL )); then
      polled="$now"
      if st="$(job_status)"; then
        case "$st" in
          running) accepted=1 ;;
          refused) [[ -z "$accepted" ]] || { echo job-gone > "$run/stopped"; kill -TERM "$pid" 2>/dev/null; exit 0; } ;;
          *) echo job-gone > "$run/stopped"; kill -TERM "$pid" 2>/dev/null; exit 0 ;;
        esac
      fi
    fi
  done
}

# Known secrets out of text that leaves the job (a branch, an issue comment):
# the job's tokens by value, and anything shaped like a GitHub, GitLab or
# Anthropic token.
redact() {
  local t v; t="$(cat)"
  for v in "${GH_TOKEN:-}" "${GITHUB_TOKEN:-}" "${GITLAB_TOKEN:-}" "${CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN:-}" "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}"; do
    if (( ${#v} >= 8 )); then t="${t//"$v"/[redacted]}"; fi
  done
  printf '%s\n' "$t" | sed -E 's/(gh[pousr]_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}|github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,}|glpat-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}|sk-ant-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,})/[redacted]/g'
}

# The end of a run's log, for an issue comment.
log_tail() { # run-dir lines
  printf '~~~~~~\n%s\n~~~~~~\n' "$(tail -n "$2" "$1/claude.log" 2>/dev/null | redact)"
}

# Where a person finds a run's record: the job and its artifact when this is a
# GitLab job, the path on this host otherwise.
run_record() { # run-dir
  if [[ -n "${CI_JOB_URL:-}" ]]; then
    local rel="${1#"${CI_PROJECT_DIR:-}"/}"
    if [[ -n "${CI_PROJECT_DIR:-}" && "$rel" != "$1" ]]; then
      echo "job $CI_JOB_URL, artifact \`$rel/claude.log\` ($CI_JOB_URL/artifacts/browse/$rel/)"
    else
      echo "job $CI_JOB_URL"
    fi
  else
    echo "\`$1/claude.log\` on $(hostname)"
  fi
}

# An issue's work in progress lives on this branch of origin, never on main.
wip_branch() { echo "looper/$REPO-$1"; } # issue
wip_on_origin() { [[ -n "$(git -C "$CHECKOUT" ls-remote origin "refs/heads/$(wip_branch "$1")")" ]]; } # issue
wip_url() { echo "https://github.com/$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO/tree/$(wip_branch "$1")"; } # issue

# Pushes everything in the checkout (local commits, staged and unstaged changes,
# new files) as one commit on HEAD to the issue's branch, through a scratch index:
# the checkout's branch, index and files stay as they are, and it fetches
# nothing (the model's own git commands run in the same checkout). With a run
# dir, the run's record rides along under .looper/ (prompt.md, the claude.log
# tail, secrets redacted), so a run killed outright still leaves its log behind.
# Pushes nothing when there is nothing to keep (no record and the checkout's
# tree is main's) or nothing new since the last checkpoint. A failed checkpoint
# is logged, never fatal.
checkpoint() { # issue why [run-dir]
  local n="$1" why="$2" run="${3:-}" step="" b; b="$(wip_branch "$n")"
  [[ -n "$run" && -s "$run/step" ]] && step="$(cat "$run/step")"
  mkdir -p "$WORK"
  ( flock 9
    cd "$CHECKOUT"
    idx="$(mktemp)"; trap 'rm -f "$idx"' EXIT
    cp "$(git rev-parse --git-path index)" "$idx" 2>/dev/null || true
    GIT_INDEX_FILE="$idx" git add -A
    # A land with no outcome yet keeps the branch land-pending (looper#38).
    if [[ -n "$run" && -f "$run/land" ]]; then
      GIT_INDEX_FILE="$idx" git update-index --add --cacheinfo "100644,$(git hash-object -w "$run/land"),.looper/land"
    else
      GIT_INDEX_FILE="$idx" git update-index --force-remove .looper/land
    fi
    if [[ -n "$run" && -f "$run/prompt.md" ]]; then
      p="$(redact < "$run/prompt.md" | git hash-object -w --stdin)"
      l="$(tail -n "$RECORD_LINES" "$run/claude.log" 2>/dev/null | redact | git hash-object -w --stdin)"
      GIT_INDEX_FILE="$idx" git update-index --add --cacheinfo "100644,$p,.looper/prompt.md" --cacheinfo "100644,$l,.looper/claude.log"
    else
      [[ "$(GIT_INDEX_FILE="$idx" git write-tree)" != "$(git rev-parse 'origin/main^{tree}')" ]] || exit 0
    fi
    tree="$(GIT_INDEX_FILE="$idx" git write-tree)"; head="$(git rev-parse HEAD)"
    last="$(git rev-parse -q --verify "refs/looper/$b" || true)"
    [[ -z "$last" || "$tree" != "$(git rev-parse "$last^{tree}")" || "$head" != "$(git rev-parse "$last^")" ]] || exit 0
    c="$(git commit-tree "$tree" -p "$head" -m "looper: work in progress on #$n ($why)" ${step:+-m "$step"})"
    prev="$(git ls-remote origin "refs/heads/$b" | cut -f1)"
    git push -q --force-with-lease="refs/heads/$b:$prev" origin "$c:refs/heads/$b"
    git update-ref "refs/looper/$b" "$c"
    log "checkpoint of #$n ($why) pushed to $b: $(git diff --shortstat origin/main "$c")"
  ) 9>"$WORK/.checkpoint.lock" || log "checkpoint of #$n ($why) failed"
}

# Checkpoints every LOOPER_CHECKPOINT_INTERVAL until killed.
checkpoint_ticker() { # issue run-dir
  local s=""
  trap 'kill "$s" 2>/dev/null; exit 0' TERM
  while :; do
    sleep "$CHECKPOINT_INTERVAL" & s=$!; wait "$s"
    checkpoint "$1" hourly "$2"
  done
}

# The work landed on main: its branch goes.
drop_wip() { # issue
  local b; b="$(wip_branch "$1")"
  if wip_on_origin "$1"; then git -C "$CHECKOUT" push -q origin --delete "$b" || log "could not delete $b"; fi
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" update-ref -d "refs/looper/$b" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# Starts the run from the issue's branch when it exists, its work as
# uncommitted changes on main (tacplus-dev/looper#9): the branch rebased on
# main; else, with no history rewritten, main merged into it; else that merge
# with its conflicts marked in the files, whose paths the prompt names to
# resolve first. Only when even the merge cannot be made does the run start
# from bare main. The work is on the checkout's BRANCH (a child's own). Prints
# what the prompt says about it; `then` is what the model does with the work.
resume() { # issue then
  local n="$1" then="$2" b msg how conflicts; b="$(wip_branch "$n")"
  wip_on_origin "$n" || return 0
  fetch "$CHECKOUT" "+refs/heads/$b:refs/remotes/origin/$b"
  msg="$(git -C "$CHECKOUT" log -1 --format=%B "origin/$b")"
  if git -C "$CHECKOUT" checkout -q --detach "origin/$b" && git -C "$CHECKOUT" rebase -q origin/main >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    how=rebased
  else
    git -C "$CHECKOUT" rebase --abort >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    git -C "$CHECKOUT" checkout -q -f --detach "origin/$b" 2>/dev/null || true
    if git -C "$CHECKOUT" merge -q --no-edit -m "looper: main merged into $b" origin/main >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      how=merged
    elif conflicts="$(git -C "$CHECKOUT" diff --name-only --diff-filter=U)" && [[ -n "$conflicts" ]] \
        && git -C "$CHECKOUT" add -A && git -C "$CHECKOUT" commit -q --no-verify -m "looper: main merged into $b, with conflicts"; then
      how=conflicted
    else
      git -C "$CHECKOUT" merge --abort >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      git -C "$CHECKOUT" checkout -q -f -B "$BRANCH" origin/main
      log "#$n: $b neither rebases on nor merges with main; starting from main" >&2
      printf 'A previous run left work in progress on the branch `%s`, but it neither rebases on nor merges with main:\nyour checkout is main. Bring that work over by hand (`git diff origin/main...origin/%s`) where it still applies.' "$b" "$b"
      printf '\nIts last checkpoint:\n\n%s\n' "$msg"
      return 0
    fi
  fi
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" checkout -q -B "$BRANCH" "$(git -C "$CHECKOUT" rev-parse HEAD)"
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" reset -q --mixed origin/main
  # The run record is not work: it stays on the branch.
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" clean -fdq -- .looper || true
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" checkout -q -- .looper 2>/dev/null || true
  case "$how" in
    rebased)
      log "resuming #$n from $b" >&2
      printf 'A previous run left work in progress on the branch `%s`. It is in your checkout, rebased on main, as\nuncommitted changes: check it, finish the issue, and %s as rule 5 says.' "$b" "$then" ;;
    merged)
      log "resuming #$n from $b (merged with main)" >&2
      printf 'A previous run left work in progress on the branch `%s`. It does not rebase on main, so it is in your\ncheckout with main merged into it, as uncommitted changes: check it, finish the issue, and\n%s as rule 5 says.' "$b" "$then" ;;
    conflicted)
      log "resuming #$n from $b (merged with main, conflicts in: $(echo $conflicts))" >&2
      printf 'A previous run left work in progress on the branch `%s`. Its merge with main conflicts with main, so it\nis in your checkout with main merged into it and the conflicts marked in the files (<<<<<<< the branch,\n>>>>>>> main), as uncommitted changes. Resolve these first, then finish the issue and\n%s as rule 5 says:\n\n' "$b" "$then"
      sed 's/.*/- `&`/' <<<"$conflicts" ;;
  esac
  printf '\nIts last checkpoint:\n\n%s\n' "$msg"
}

# The issue waits on other open issues: the run's outcome file names them as
# `Blocked-by:` lines, whether it pushed a fix whose gates wait or rightly
# pushed nothing (looper#22). Adds the lines its body lacks and prints them
# all; fails, adding none, when the outcome names none, or any it names is
# closed, does not exist or is the issue itself.
outcome_blocked() { # issue run-dir
  local n="$1" refs r b state body add="" lines=()
  [[ -f "$2/outcome" ]] || return 1
  refs="$(parse_blockers "$REPO" < "$2/outcome" | awk '!seen[$0]++')"
  [[ -n "$refs" ]] || return 1
  body="$(gh issue view "$n" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --json body --jq .body)" || return 1
  while read -r r b; do
    [[ "$r $b" != "$REPO $n" ]] || return 1
    state="$(gh issue view "$b" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$r" --json state --jq .state)" || return 1
    [[ "$state" == OPEN ]] || return 1
    if [[ "$r" == "$REPO" ]]; then lines+=("Blocked-by: #$b"); else lines+=("Blocked-by: $LOOPER_ORG/$r#$b"); fi
    parse_blockers "$REPO" <<<"$body" | grep -qx "$r $b" || add+="${lines[-1]}"$'\n'
  done <<<"$refs"
  if [[ -n "$add" ]]; then
    gh issue edit "$n" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --body "$body

${add%$'\n'}" >/dev/null || return 1
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "${lines[@]}"
}

# The issue flow (looper#14): the issue's kind from its labels, `feature` when
# it has both; nothing when it has neither (or they cannot be read).
issue_kind() { # issue
  local l; l="$(gh issue view "$1" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --json labels --jq '.labels[].name')" || return 0
  if grep -qx "$LABEL_FEATURE" <<<"$l"; then echo "$LABEL_FEATURE"
  elif grep -qx "$LABEL_BUG" <<<"$l"; then echo "$LABEL_BUG"; fi
}

# A feature is built only when a ROADMAP.md links it: this repository's (a
# bare #N counts) or the platform's (tacplus-dev/roadmap). 0: linked; 1: not;
# 2: the platform's cannot be read.
on_roadmap() { # issue
  if [[ -f "$CHECKOUT/ROADMAP.md" ]] && links_issue "$REPO" "$1" own < "$CHECKOUT/ROADMAP.md"; then return 0; fi
  local p; p="$(platform_file ROADMAP.md)" || return 2
  links_issue "$REPO" "$1" <<<"$p" || return 1
}

# Ends the issue's run without working it: the label, and a comment saying why.
hold() { # issue label why
  gh issue edit "$1" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --remove-label "$LABEL_IN_PROGRESS" --add-label "$2" >/dev/null || true
  gh issue comment "$1" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --body "$3" >/dev/null || true
  log "#$1 held ($2)"
}

# An unlabelled issue: the model classifies it, in a comment and a label, and
# does nothing else. Its record is the run's classify/.
classify() { # issue run-dir limit
  local n="$1" dir="$2/classify"; mkdir -p "$dir"
  cat > "$dir/prompt.md" <<PR
You are the looper of $LOOPER_ORG/$REPO, classifying issue #$n before it is worked.
Change no file and push nothing. Read it: \`gh issue view $n --repo $LOOPER_ORG/$REPO --comments\`. Every issue
is a bug or a feature ($ISSUE_FLOW):
- **bug:** the code does not do what it already promises: a defect, a vulnerability, a broken gate, a stale pin.
- **feature:** new behaviour, new surface, a changed contract.
Comment on the issue which it is and why, in a sentence or two (\`Classified as **bug**: ...\`), then label it:
\`gh issue edit $n --repo $LOOPER_ORG/$REPO --add-label $LABEL_BUG\` (or \`$LABEL_FEATURE\`). Nothing else: the looper
works it by that label.
PR
  run_claude "$dir/prompt.md" "$dir/claude.log" "$(( $3 < 1800 ? $3 : 1800 ))" "$dir" || log "classifying #$n exited non-zero"
}

# A feature that no ROADMAP.md links is not built: the model proposes its
# design, on the issue and in PROPOSALS.md, and the issue waits on a decision.
propose() { # issue run-dir limit
  local n="$1" run="$2"
  cat > "$run/prompt.md" <<PR
You are the looper of $LOOPER_ORG/$REPO, proposing a design for issue #$n. Your working directory is a worktree
of this repository on its own branch, $BRANCH, at origin/main. Read the issue: \`gh issue view $n --repo $LOOPER_ORG/$REPO --comments\`,
and the documents it cites.

It is a feature, and no ROADMAP.md links it (neither this repository's nor tacplus-dev/roadmap's), so it is not
built ($ISSUE_FLOW): a feature needs design, Jody's approval and a ROADMAP.md entry first. Do not build it.
1. Comment on the issue with a design proposal: what and why, the approach and the alternatives, the surface or
   contract it changes, the risks, its size, and the questions Jody must answer.
2. Add it to PROPOSALS.md, in that file's format (what, why, links: this issue). Commit only PROPOSALS.md on
   $BRANCH, with a message ending with \`Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>\`, then land it on main:
   $(land_command "$n"). Change no other file; never ROADMAP.md.
The looper labels the issue $LABEL_DECISION when you are done.
PR
  run_claude "$run/prompt.md" "$run/claude.log" "$(( $3 < 3600 ? $3 : 3600 ))" "$run" || log "proposing #$n exited non-zero"
  hold "$n" "$LABEL_DECISION" "This is a \`$LABEL_FEATURE\` that no ROADMAP.md links (\`$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO:ROADMAP.md\`, \`$LOOPER_ORG/roadmap:ROADMAP.md\`), so it is not built ($ISSUE_FLOW): its design proposal is on this issue and in PROPOSALS.md. Once Jody approves the design and an agent adds it to a ROADMAP.md, linking this issue, remove \`$LABEL_DECISION\` and the looper builds it. Run record: $(run_record "$run")."
}

# The newest release on main (a vX.Y.Z tag main contains), or nothing.
main_release() {
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" fetch -q --tags origin 2>/dev/null || true
  git -C "$CHECKOUT" tag --merged origin/main -l 'v*' | grep -E '^v[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+$' | sort -V | tail -n 1 || true
}

# The prompt's rule 7, by the issue's kind.
kind_rule() { # kind
  if [[ "$1" == "$LABEL_FEATURE" ]]; then
    printf '%s\n' "7. This issue is a feature on a ROADMAP.md ($ISSUE_FLOW): it ships in the next minor release," \
      "   from main only: no backport, and no patch release."
    return 0
  fi
  local v; v="$(main_release)"
  printf '%s\n' "7. This issue is a bug ($ISSUE_FLOW; tacplus-dev/tacplus docs/VERSIONING.md): fix it, and ship the" \
    "   fix as a patch release on every supported release line that has it."
  if [[ -n "$v" ]]; then
    local x="${v#v}"
    printf '%s\n' "   Release the patch on main: once the fix is pushed and its gates pass, tag \`v${x%.*}.$(( ${x##*.} + 1 ))\` at it" \
      "   (this repository's newest release on main is \`$v\`) and push the tag, the way the repository releases (its" \
      "   release workflow, justfile or CONTRIBUTING). If main has taken features since \`$v\` (they belong to the next" \
      "   minor), do not cut the patch from main: it goes to release/${x%.*} as a backport, below."
  else
    echo "   This repository has no release on main yet (no vX.Y.Z tag): there is no patch to release."
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "   A repository with release/MAJOR.MINOR branches: the fix is also backported as a pull request against each" \
    "   release branch whose .0 tag is under twelve months old (never pushed to one directly), whose description" \
    "   names the patch tag to cut on that branch once it merges."
}

# How a child lands its commits on main (looper#32), for its prompt.
land_command() { # issue
  if [[ -n "$(land_gates)" ]]; then
    echo "\`$LOOPER_SELF --repo $REPO --issue $1 --land\`"
  else
    echo "\`$LOOPER_SELF --repo $REPO --issue $1 --land --gates \"<the command you verified with>\"\` (this repository has no justfile, so name its gates)"
  fi
}

# What every prompt that may open an issue says about opening one.
opening_rule() { # [its number in the prompt]
  cat <<EOF
${1:+$1 }Before you open any issue (a follow-up, say), read this repository's ROADMAP.md and the platform's: tacplus-dev/roadmap's
   ROADMAP.md and items/ (\`gh api repos/$LOOPER_ORG/roadmap/contents/ROADMAP.md -H 'Accept: application/vnd.github.raw'\`).
   A feature already on a roadmap gets no new feature issue: open one, labelled \`$LABEL_FEATURE\`, asking for that
   entry to be prioritized, linking it and saying why now. Any other issue is labelled \`$LABEL_BUG\` (the code does
   not do what it already promises) or \`$LABEL_FEATURE\` (new behaviour), and a feature is never \`$LABEL_APPROVED\`: it
   needs design, Jody's approval and a ROADMAP.md entry, and it goes into PROPOSALS.md ($ISSUE_FLOW).
EOF
}

# A child's outcome, for its parent (looper#32): closed, blocked,
# needs-decision, proposed, timeout, land-pending, land-deferred, landed or ended (resumes), stuck or
# job-gone.
report() { # issue outcome
  mkdir -p "$WORK/results"; echo "$2" > "$WORK/results/$1"
}

# A child's run of an issue, in its worktree (the parent made it).
work_issue() { # issue
  local n="$1" run limit resumed last_comment
  run="$RUNS/$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)-issue-$n"; mkdir -p "$run"
  log "taking $LOOPER_ORG/$REPO#$n ($run, in $CHECKOUT on $BRANCH)"
  gh issue edit "$n" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --add-label "$LABEL_IN_PROGRESS" >/dev/null
  limit="$(run_limit "$n")"
  # A limit that would outlast the job is capped at the parent's deadline
  # (looper#35): the run stops by itself, checkpointed and its outcome
  # commented, before GitLab kills the job (an issue's `Timeout:` line can
  # change after the job was sized for it).
  local deadline left; deadline="$(job_deadline)"
  if [[ -n "$deadline" ]] && left=$(( deadline - $(date +%s) )) && (( limit > left )); then
    (( left > 0 )) || left=1   # never 0: `timeout 0` would not stop it
    log "#$n: limit $(hours "$limit") capped at $(hours "$left") by this job"
    gh issue comment "$n" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --body "The looper of $REPO starts this issue with its limit $(hours "$limit") capped at $(hours "$left") by this job (${CI_JOB_URL:-$CI_JOB_ID}), which ends sooner: the run stops by itself before then, its work checkpointed, and the next run resumes it." >/dev/null || true
    limit="$left"
  fi
  # Bug or feature (looper#14): an unlabelled issue is classified first; a
  # feature no ROADMAP.md links is proposed, never built.
  ensure_kind_labels "$REPO"
  local kind; kind="$(issue_kind "$n")"
  if [[ -z "$kind" ]]; then classify "$n" "$run" "$limit"; kind="$(issue_kind "$n")"; fi
  if [[ -z "$kind" ]]; then
    hold "$n" "$LABEL_DECISION" "This issue is labelled neither \`$LABEL_BUG\` nor \`$LABEL_FEATURE\`, and the looper could not classify it ($ISSUE_FLOW). A person labels it \`$LABEL_BUG\` or \`$LABEL_FEATURE\` and removes \`$LABEL_DECISION\`. Run record: $(run_record "$run")."
    report "$n" "$LABEL_DECISION"; return 0
  fi
  if [[ "$kind" == "$LABEL_FEATURE" ]]; then
    local on=0; on_roadmap "$n" || on=$?
    case "$on" in
      0) ;;
      1) propose "$n" "$run" "$limit"; report "$n" proposed; return 0 ;;
      *) hold "$n" "$LABEL_STUCK" "The looper could not read \`$LOOPER_ORG/roadmap:ROADMAP.md\`, so it cannot tell whether this feature is on a roadmap ($ISSUE_FLOW): it is neither built nor proposed. Marked $LABEL_STUCK for a person to look at."
         report "$n" stuck; return 0 ;;
    esac
  fi
  # A land an earlier job deferred, or that its end cut off, is landed first.
  local prior_rc=0; land_prior "$n" "$run" || prior_rc=$?
  if (( prior_rc == LAND_DEFERRED )); then
    gh issue edit "$n" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --remove-label "$LABEL_IN_PROGRESS" >/dev/null || true
    report "$n" land-deferred; log "#$n: its land is deferred again; the next run lands it first"; return 0
  fi
  resumed="$(resume "$n" "commit it and land it")"
  if [[ -n "$resumed" ]]; then
    # The step it resumes from: the same step again, nothing landed, is no progress.
    git -C "$CHECKOUT" log -1 --format=%b "origin/$(wip_branch "$n")" > "$run/resumed-step" 2>/dev/null || true
    last_comment="$(gh issue view "$n" --repo "$LOOPER_ORG/$REPO" --json comments --jq '.comments[-1].body // ""')" || true
    if [[ -n "$last_comment" ]]; then resumed="$resumed

The issue's last comment:

$last_comment"; fi
    resumed="
$resumed
"
  fi
  cat > "$run/prompt.md" <<PR
You are the looper of $LOOPER_ORG/$REPO, working issue #$n. Your working directory is a worktree of this
repository on its own branch, $BRANCH, at origin/main: other issues of this repository may be worked at the
same time, each in its own worktree. Read the issue first: \`gh issue view $n --repo $LOOPER_ORG/$REPO --comments\`.
It was approved by someone with write access; issues it links to and the documents it cites (decision
records, IDENTITY.md, CONTRIBUTING, docs/) are your context. Read them before changing anything.
$resumed
Rules:
1. Do what the issue asks, completely: its acceptance criteria are the definition of done. If it asks for
   something you believe is wrong, or needs a decision you may not make (a public contract the issue does
   not settle, a change in another repository), comment on the issue with the question and your
   recommendation, add the label $LABEL_DECISION, and stop without committing.
2. Tests first where the change has behaviour: write the test that fails, then make it pass. Surgical
   changes, the existing style, no hacks. Generated code is never edited by hand: change the generator or
